fix-ecc-point-coordinate-range: reject ECC point coordinates not below the field prime

Classification: spec-deviation bug fix.

Specification (TPM 2.0 Library, Part 1, "ECDH" and the key-exchange and
commit operations, which follow NIST SP 800-56A; SP 800-56A Rev. 3, 5.6.2.3.3
"ECC Full Public-Key Validation Routine", step 2):

  "Verify that xQ and yQ are integers in the interval [0, p - 1] in the case
  that q is an odd prime p ..."

A point on an elliptic curve over GF(p) has coordinates that are field
elements. BnIsPointOnCurve() only checks the curve equation modulo p, so it
accepts (x + p, y) or (x, y + p) whenever the larger integer still fits the
TPM2B_ECC_PARAMETER of the curve (e.g. any BN P-638 coordinate, or a small x
of NIST P-224), and the TPM then works with a non-canonical encoding of the
point (TPM2_LoadExternal, TPM2_ECDH_ZGen, TPM2_Commit, ...). The patch
rejects coordinates that are not below p, as tpm-rs does.

diff --git a/TPMCmd/tpm/cryptolibs/TpmBigNum/BnMath.c b/TPMCmd/tpm/cryptolibs/TpmBigNum/BnMath.c
index 1afa690..cff7321 100644
--- a/TPMCmd/tpm/cryptolibs/TpmBigNum/BnMath.c
+++ b/TPMCmd/tpm/cryptolibs/TpmBigNum/BnMath.c
@@ -453,6 +453,11 @@ BOOL BnIsPointOnCurve(pointConst Q, const TPMBN_ECC_CURVE_CONSTANTS* C)
     BN_VAR(left, (MAX_ECC_KEY_BITS * 2));
     bigConst prime = BnCurveGetPrime(C);
     //
+    // tpm-rs differential testing -- C-ref spec deviation fix (NOT determinism):
+    // the coordinates of a point are field elements, integers in [0, p - 1]
+    // (SP 800-56A Rev. 3, 5.6.2.3.3, ECC Full Public-Key Validation, step 2).
+    if(BnUnsignedCmp(Q->x, prime) >= 0 || BnUnsignedCmp(Q->y, prime) >= 0)
+        return FALSE;
     // Show that point is on the curve y^2 = x^3 + ax + b;
     // Or y^2 = x(x^2 + a) + b
     // y^2
